        fp: "A commercial boat dealership had an unlimited supply of recreational ski boats available from its manufacturer at a wholesale cost of $40,000 per boat[cite: 55]. A buyer entered the dealership and executed a signed written contract to purchase an in-stock, standard-production ski boat for $50,000 cash, with delivery scheduled for the following Friday[cite: 55]. Two days later, the buyer repudiated the agreement in writing and refused to tender payment[cite: 55]. On Friday morning, another customer entered the dealership, saw the exact boat that had been allocated to the first buyer, and purchased it for the identical retail price of $50,000 cash[cite: 55]. The dealership incurred no extra selling or storage expenses in completing the second transaction[cite: 55]. The dealership sued the first buyer for $10,000 in lost profits[cite: 55]. The first buyer argued that because the dealership resold the exact boat for the full contract price without incurring incidental losses, the dealership suffered zero actual damages[cite: 55].",
