        exp: "Rule: Under UCC § 2-206(1)(b), an order or other offer to buy goods for prompt or current shipment is construed as inviting acceptance either by a prompt promise to ship or by the prompt or current shipment of conforming or nonconforming goods[cite: 55]. HOWEVER, the statute contains a vital exception: 'a shipment of non-conforming goods does not constitute an acceptance if the seller seasonably notifies the buyer that the shipment is offered only as an ACCOMMODATION to the buyer.'[cite: 55] When a seller ships nonconforming goods and seasonably notifies the buyer that they are sent merely as an accommodation, the shipment does NOT operate as an acceptance and breach; rather, it constitutes a COUNTEROFFER[cite: 55]. The buyer is free to accept or reject the counteroffer, but the seller is NOT liable for breach of contract because no contract was ever accepted (Option B)[cite: 55]. Option A is incorrect because the accommodation notice prevents the shipment from operating as an acceptance/breach[cite: 55]. Option C is incorrect because the accommodation rule applies to any order or offer to buy goods for prompt shipment[cite: 55]. Option D is incorrect because the UCC perfect tender rule requires exact aesthetic conformity, but liability is avoided via the counteroffer mechanism[cite: 55]."
