        fp: "A homeowner contracted in writing with a licensed residential general contractor to construct a timber-framed outdoor patio pavilion for $30,000, payable upon completion on June 1[cite: 55]. Under the agreement, the contractor was to purchase and install premium kiln-dried western red cedar lumber[cite: 55]. The contract contained the following clause: 'This agreement constitutes the final, complete, and exclusive expression of the parties' contract; no prior oral or written representations shall vary its terms.'[cite: 55] Before signing the written contract, the contractor orally stated to the homeowner: 'Our lumber distributor's wholesale prices fluctuate; if lumber prices increase by more than 10% before May 1, the total contract price will be increased by $3,000.'[cite: 55] The homeowner orally replied: 'That is understood and agreeable.'[cite: 55] The written agreement omitted any mention of lumber price escalations[cite: 55]. Lumber prices increased by 15% in April[cite: 55]. The contractor completed the pavilion on June 1 in full conformity with specifications, but submitted an invoice for $33,000[cite: 55]. The homeowner tendered $30,000, refusing to pay the additional $3,000[cite: 55]. The contractor sued to recover the $3,000, seeking to introduce evidence of the prior oral price-escalation agreement[cite: 55].",
