        topic: "Mixed",
        fp: "An industrial tool manufacturing company entered into a written contract with an export trading firm to manufacture and deliver 1,000 specialized pneumatic impact wrenches at $50 each ($50,000 total)[cite: 55]. The contract specified delivery on or before October 1[cite: 55]. The contract contained the following clause: 'Seller warrants that all tools delivered hereunder shall be free from defects in material and workmanship for a period of one year. Seller disclaims all other warranties, express or implied.'[cite: 55] On October 1, the manufacturer delivered 1,000 wrenches to the export firm's warehouse[cite: 55]. The export firm inspected the tools and discovered that the internal drive anvils were made of cast iron rather than hardened forged alloy steel as required by the technical blueprints, rendering the wrenches brittle and unsuitable for heavy industrial use[cite: 55]. The export firm immediately notified the manufacturer that it was rejecting the shipment for breach of warranty and demanded return of its advance deposit[cite: 55]. The manufacturer asserted that its written disclaimer extinguished the implied warranty of merchantability, and offered to replace only those anvils that actually broke during the one-year warranty period[cite: 55].",
